(0) (1a) and (1b) are completely trivial problems. The + sign and the - sig=
n are NOT mistakes. If one does not know enough algebra to fill in the blan=
ks CORRECTLY with the actual signs on the exam AND respecting equality, one=
 should NOT pass Math 163. This is a modest attempt on my part to 'brain-dr=
y' you from the harmful brain-washing that you have received in the form of=
 multiple-choice tests which, along with excessive doses of television and =
now 'social media' practically from birth, are responsible for the observed=
 stupor in c problems where very many students submit identical nonsense wi=
th no rhyme or reason.

(1) (2a) is easy. (2b) is not entirely easy. But one MUST follow the format=
ting in the Solved Exam exactly. (2c) requires one to prove one's conjectur=
e inductively. Induction has a very specific meaning in this class that inc=
ludes a particular implementation and formatting. The format is exhibited i=
n the Solved Exam in other parts. The format also exists in the Notes on Re=
cursion and Induction, for instance, on pages 11 to 14 and also later. If o=
ne engages in any form of change, variation, or omission from what is done =
in the Notes, one is almost certain to get 0 as many did on Exam 1C.

(2) (3a) requires one to understand recursion and induction as defined in t=
he notes thoroughly. One must follow the formatting of the Solved Exam exac=
tly.

(3) (3b) requires one to understand and adapt the proof in the Notes or in =
the Solved Exam (which does exactly that). One must make certain that one f=
ollows the exact logic, uses the correct numbers and correct properties. If=
 one does not understand this proof completely, one should not study mathem=
atics at all.

(4) (1c) requires one to think very hard and come up with proper interpreta=
tions from fragments from one's own experience. PLEASE DO NOT WRITE TOTAL N=
ONSENSE.

(5) (3c) requires one to produce a recursive scheme for digits in particula=
r locations, NOT truncated decimals. Read the instructions VERY CAREFULLY. =
Otherwise one may lose more points than a problem is worth.

